## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical data ingestion, de-dup logic, and per-tenant isolation in a real GCP pipeline, a common beginner-to-intermediate scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Dataflow (Beam) pipelines, Pub/Sub integration, BigQuery partitioning and clustering
- Idempotent upserts with event_id, per-tenant isolation via tenant_id
- Dead-letter handling and archival strategy, basic monitoring
- Rollback using staged data and replay window, testing plan

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: dedupe by event_id within a fixed window
function dedupe(events) {
  const seen = new Set();
  return events.filter(e => {
    if (seen.has(e.event_id)) return false;
    seen.add(e.event_id);
    return true;
  });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test idempotent writes and rollback reliably?
- What are the cost implications of partitioning by day and clustering by tenant_id?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests experience designing scalable, governed data platforms with multi-tenant isolation, data contracts, and end-to-end lineage on GCP. It also probes handling late-arriving data, PII masking, and per-tenant rollback plans.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant isolation in BigQuery with Authorized Views
- Data contracts and lineage via Data Catalog
- Ingestion-time PII masking with DLP
- Event-time processing and late-arrivals in Dataflow
- Cross-region replication and immutable audit logs
- Schema evolution and per-tenant rollback strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: ingest path with DLP masking before storage
const ingest = (record) => {
  const masked = dlp.maskPII(record);
  pubsub.publish(masked);
};
```

